	Configure a signal path to perform echo cancellation
apifn	sm_path_echocancel
params	sm_path_echocancel_parms cancelp
	The signal path to be configured
field	tSMPathId path in
	Enables (non-zero) or disables (zero) echo cancellation of the
	input signal.
field   tSM_INT enable in
	The datafeed which is the reference signal. Only used if
ref	enable
	is non-zero.
field   tSMDatafeedId reference in
	Enables (non-zero) or disables (zero) non-linear processing. 
	The exact behaviour of the non-linear processing module is controlled
	by
ref	mode
	. Negative 
	values represent the lower limit in dBm0. Positive values cause 
	the default value of -42dBm0 to be used.
	Only used if
ref	enable
	is non-zero.
field   tSM_INT non_linear in
	Selects the non-linear echo cancellation mode to be used. Only used if
ref	non_linear
	is non-zero.
enumstart kSMPathEchoNonLinearMode
	Uses non-linear processing to suppress the signal whenever
	echo or background noise are dominant. This mode of operation
	is suitable for large-scale conferencing and/or IVR applications,
	where it is important that the signal is completely muted whenever
	the caller is not speaking.
enum	kSMPathEchoNonLinearModeMuting
	Uses non-linear processing to replace the signal with comfort noise
	whenever echo might otherwise be audible. This mode of operation is
	most appropriate to IP gateway applications where background noise is
	desirable to maintain the illusion of continuity.
enum	kSMPathEchoNonLinearModeCNG
enumend mode in
	Enables (non-zero) or disables (zero) the use of automatic gain
	control on the input signal.
	Only used if
ref	enable
	is non-zero.
field   tSM_INT use_agc in
	Enables (non-zero) or disables (zero) holding the gain fixed at its
	current value. This can be used after the AGC gain factor has adjusted
	itself to prevent further changes.
	Only used if
ref	enable
	is non-zero.
field   tSM_INT fix_agc in
	If non-zero, sets the span (in milliseconds) of the echo cancellation.
	A side effect of this is that the input conditioning may be re-initialised.
	Only used if
ref	enable
	is non-zero.
field   tSM_INT span in
	If non-zero, sets delay (in milliseconds) before the reference signal is presented 
	to echo cancellation reference input (allowing application to compensate for time 
	it takes reference signal to reach the TDM circuit, so avoiding the effective span 
	being diminished by this amount). Input conditioning is re-initialised. To set a 
	zero delay, use the value -1. If zero, the delay is unchanged and input conditioning
	is not re-initialised. The default delay is zero milliseconds. 
field	tSM_INT delay in
enumstart kSMPathEchoToneAction
	No special action on occurence of tones.
enum	kSMPathEchoToneActionNone
	TTY tones are expected - when detected action taken to mute TTY echo but passthrough non-echo TTY.
enum	kSMPathEchoTonetActionMuteTTY
enumend tone_action in
unstruct
	Configures echo cancellation on a signal path. The
	input is examined for an echo of the
ref	reference
	signal, and any echo found is suppressed.
	<p>
	Non-linear echo cancellation can operate in two modes. The default is 
	non-linear echo cancellation with muting, which is suitable for large-scale 
	conferencing and/or IVR applications, where it is important that the signal 
	is completely muted whenever the caller is not speaking.
	<p>
	Non-linear echo cancellation with comfort noise generation (CNG) never mutes 
	the signal. Instead it replaces any residual traces of echo with "comfort 
	noise". This mode of operation is most appropriate to IP gateway applications 
	where background noise is desirable to maintain the illusion of continuity.
	<p>
	If tone_action is set to mute TTY, the echo cancellation algorithm will expect to 
	encounter TTY tones on its primary input and its reference. 
	Its output will be muted whenever such tones are present on its reference and for a 
	short time afterwards (thus muting echoes of tones). It will pass though TTY tones 
	present on its primary input at other times. This behaviour should only be enabled 
	if TTY tones are expected in the signal path. It would normally be used with 
	non-linear mode disabled.
	<p>
	Requires the module
modref  echocan.
explain
	0
retval

	Configure a signal path to perform automatic gain control
apifn	sm_path_agc
params	sm_path_agc_parms agcp
	The signal path to be configured
field	tSMPathId path in
	Indicator of whether automatic gain control is to be enabled
	(non-zero) or disabled (zero).
field   tSM_INT agc in
	The volume adjustment (dBm0).
field	tSM_INT volume in
unstruct
	Configures automatic gain control and fixed volume adjustment on
	a signal path. The
ref	agc
	parameter controls whether or not automatic gain control is
	applied to the signal. If
ref	agc
	is non-zero then automatic gain control is applied. The
	volume adjustment specified is performed after the AGC and
	has a range of at least -24 to +8 dB. Volume adjustment is
	always applied, even if AGC is disabled.
	<p>
	Requires the module
modref  gainbg.
explain
	0
retval
error   ERR_SM_DEVERR - device error

